---
import SystemPage from '../../components/v2/SystemPage.astro';
import { imagery } from '../../content/imagery';

const v2 = imagery.v2;
---

<SystemPage
	title="Asphalt shingle roofs"
	heroEyebrow="Residential · materials"
	heroHeading="Asphalt shingle.<br />The default for East Texas."
	heroLede="Architectural and laminated shingles from GAF, Owens Corning, CertainTeed, Atlas, and Malarkey. The right answer for most East Texas homes — and we say so honestly."
	heroPhoto={v2.pageHeros.asphaltShingle}
	heroCta={{ label: 'Get my free inspection', href: '/free-inspection' }}
	bleedPhoto={v2.bleeds.workmanship}
	bleedHeading="Most residential roofs<br />install in one day."
	overview={{
		eyebrow: 'Why asphalt',
		heading: 'It works for the climate, the budget, and the timeline.',
		body: "Asphalt shingles are the default for residential roofing in East Texas for good reason — they handle our heat, hail, and humidity at a price homeowners can absorb on a 20-year cycle, and the install fits inside one day for most houses. Architectural laminated shingles in particular have closed most of the visual gap with metal and tile while staying inside a residential budget. We install GAF, Owens Corning, CertainTeed, Atlas, and Malarkey — manufacturer pick depends on the warranty Heritage carries with each."
	}}
	keyPoints={[
		{
			heading: 'Architectural vs 3-tab.',
			body: 'Architectural (laminated) shingles are the standard now — two layers of asphalt mat creates depth, dimensionality, and roughly 2x the wind/impact rating of 3-tab. 3-tab is largely a budget tier for shorter-stay rentals or repairs that need to match an old roof. We default-spec architectural and price 3-tab if asked.',
			bullets: ['Architectural is the new default', '110–130 mph wind ratings standard', 'Class 4 impact options (Pinnacle Impact, NorthGate, Northgate ClimateFlex)', 'Algae resistance built in'],
			photo: v2.materialTiles.asphalt,
			photoSide: 'left',
		},
		{
			heading: 'How long it actually lasts.',
			body: "Manufacturer warranties read 30 years, lifetime, or limited-lifetime. Real-world East Texas service life on a quality architectural shingle is 20–25 years. The variables are attic ventilation (#1 cause of premature failure we see), hail history, and color (lighter shingles age slower in our heat). We document ventilation on every inspection.",
			bullets: ['20–25 yr real-world service in East Texas', 'Attic ventilation is the #1 longevity factor', 'Lighter colors age slower in our heat', 'Hail history matters — we account for it'],
			photo: v2.bleeds.workmanship,
			photoSide: 'right',
		},
	]}
	stats={[
		{ value: '20–25', label: 'Years real-world service · East TX' },
		{ value: '130', label: 'Mph wind rating · architectural', numericValue: 130, suffix: ' mph' },
		{ value: 'Class 4', label: 'Impact rating · upgrade tier' },
		{ value: '1 day', label: 'Typical install timeline' },
	]}
	projectPhotos={[v2.projects[0], v2.projects[1], v2.projects[6], v2.projects[7]]}
	projectCaptions={['Architectural re-roof, Tyler', 'Insurance replacement, Longview', 'Tear-off + decking, Marshall', 'Color match repair, Kilgore']}
	faq={[
		{ q: 'Which manufacturer is best?', a: "<p>The honest answer: there's no single best. GAF Timberline HDZ, Owens Corning Duration, CertainTeed Landmark, Atlas Pinnacle Pristine, and Malarkey Highlander are all solid architectural shingles in the same performance band. The decision usually comes down to which warranty Heritage carries with that manufacturer and which color matches the house. We tell you which one we'd put on our own house and why.</p>" },
		{ q: 'How much does a roof actually cost?', a: '<p>Residential asphalt re-roofs in East Texas land in a wide band depending on square footage, slope, decking condition, ventilation work, and material tier. We won\'t put a number on this page — every roof is different. The free inspection ends with a written quote with the actual number.</p>' },
		{ q: 'Class 4 impact — worth it?', a: "<p>If you've had hail damage in the last 10 years, yes. Insurance discounts on Class 4 shingles in hail-prone areas often pay back the upgrade cost over the roof's life. If you haven't had hail, the math is closer — Class 4 shingles cost more up front and the discount only matters if your insurer offers it. We tell you both numbers on the proposal.</p>" },
		{ q: 'Tear-off or overlay?', a: '<p>Tear-off, almost always. Overlay (installing new shingles over old) saves tear-off cost but locks in any decking problems, voids most warranty coverage, and traps an old layer of failure underneath. We only overlay on jobs where the existing roof is essentially new and the homeowner accepts the warranty trade-offs in writing.</p>' },
		{ q: 'What about the warranty?', a: '<p>Manufacturer warranty (30 yr to limited-lifetime) plus Heritage workmanship warranty on the install. Specific term lengths and any extended coverage land on the proposal.</p>' },
		{ q: 'Will my premium go up after a claim?', a: "<p>No. Insurance premiums in Texas don't go up based on a single weather-related claim filed properly. We talk you through that on the inspection if it's a concern. Note: this is general guidance, not insurance advice — your carrier and your specific policy govern.</p>" },
	]}
	ctaHeading="Free inspection.<br />Real quote in a day."
	ctaLede="Same-day or next-day quote. Real person on the ladder, not a salesperson."
	ctaPhoto={v2.pageHeros.freeInspection}
	ctaPrimary={{ label: 'Get my free inspection', href: '/free-inspection' }}
	promises={['Insurance accepted', 'Workmanship + material warranties', 'Free inspections']}
/>
